{"task_id": "HumanEval/39", "solution": "```python\ndef _is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given integer is a prime number.\n    It handles numbers less than or equal to 1 and even numbers efficiently.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num == 2:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    # Check for factors from 3 up to the square root of num, skipping even numbers.\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= num:\n        if num % i == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 2\n    return True\n\ndef _generate_fibonacci_numbers():\n    \"\"\"Generates an infinite sequence of Fibonacci numbers.\n    The sequence starts with F(0)=0, F(1)=1, F(2)=1, F(3)=2, and so on.\n    \"\"\"\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    while True:\n        yield a\n        a, b = b, a + b\n\ndef prime_fib(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    prime_fib returns n-th number that is a Fibonacci number and it's also prime.\n    >>> prime_fib(1)\n    2\n    >>> prime_fib(2)\n    3\n    >>> prime_fib(3)\n    5\n    >>> prime_fib(4)\n    13\n    >>> prime_fib(5)\n    89\n    \"\"\"\n    prime_count = 0\n    fib_gen = _generate_fibonacci_numbers()\n\n    while True:\n        fib_num = next(fib_gen)\n        # According to the design, only numbers greater than 1 are considered for primality.\n        if fib_num > 1 and _is_prime(fib_num):\n            prime_count += 1\n            if prime_count == n:\n                return fib_num\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/46", "solution": "```python\n# src/fib4_calculator.py\n\ndef fib4(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the n-th element of the Fib4 number sequence iteratively.\n    Handles base cases and sums the four preceding elements for n > 3 without recursion.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n    if n == 2:\n        return 2\n    if n == 3:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize with the first four base cases: fib4(0), fib4(1), fib4(2), fib4(3).\n    # This list acts as a sliding window of the last four computed values.\n    last_four = [0, 0, 2, 0]\n\n    # Iterate from the 4th index up to n to compute subsequent Fib4 numbers.\n    for _ in range(4, n + 1):\n        # Calculate the next Fib4 number by summing the current four values.\n        next_fib = sum(last_four)\n        # Remove the oldest value and add the newly computed value to maintain the sliding window.\n        last_four.pop(0)\n        last_four.append(next_fib)\n\n    # The last element in the list is fib4(n).\n    return last_four[-1]\n\n```\n```python\n# src/main.py\n\nimport sys\n# Import the fib4 function from the fib4_calculator module within the 'src' package.\nfrom .fib4_calculator import fib4\n\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"\n    The main entry point for the Fib4 Sequence Calculator application.\n    Parses command-line arguments, computes the Fib4 number, and displays the result.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Check for the correct number of command-line arguments.\n    if len(sys.argv) != 2:\n        print(\"Usage: python src/main.py <n>\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n\n    try:\n        # Attempt to convert the input argument to a non-negative integer.\n        n = int(sys.argv[1])\n        if n < 0:\n            print(\"Error: n must be a non-negative integer.\")\n            sys.exit(1)\n    except ValueError:\n        # Handle cases where the input is not a valid integer.\n        print(\"Error: n must be an integer.\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n\n    # Compute the n-th Fib4 number using the fib4 function.\n    result = fib4(n)\n\n    # Print the computed result to standard output.\n    print(result)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n\n```\n```python\n# src/__init__.py\n# This file marks the 'src' directory as a Python package.\n\n```"}
