{"task_id": "HumanEval/67", "solution": "```python\ndef fruit_distribution(s, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    In this task, you will be given a string that represents a number of apples and oranges\n    that are distributed in a basket of fruit this basket contains apples, oranges, and mango fruits.\n    Given the string that represents the total number of the oranges and apples and an integer that\n    represent the total number of the fruits in the basket return the number of the mango fruits in the basket.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Split the input string to extract counts.\n    parts = s.split(' ')\n\n    # Extract apple and orange count strings and convert them to integers.\n    num_apples = int(parts[0])\n    num_oranges = int(parts[3])\n\n    # Calculate the total number of apples and oranges.\n    total_known_fruits = num_apples + num_oranges\n\n    # Subtract known fruits from the total to find mangoes.\n    num_mangoes = n - total_known_fruits\n\n    return num_mangoes\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/67", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef fruit_distribution(s,n):\n    \"\"\"\n    In this task, you will be given a string that represents a number of apples and oranges\n    that are distributed in a basket of fruit this basket contains\n    apples, oranges, and mango fruits. Given the string that represents the total number of\n    the oranges and apples and an integer that represent the total number of the fruits\n    in the basket return the number of the mango fruits in the basket.\n    for examble:\n    fruit_distribution(\"5 apples and 6 oranges\", 19) ->19 - 5 - 6 = 8\n    fruit_distribution(\"0 apples and 1 oranges\",3) -> 3 - 0 - 1 = 2\n    fruit_distribution(\"2 apples and 3 oranges\", 100) -> 100 - 2 - 3 = 95\n    fruit_distribution(\"100 apples and 1 oranges\",120) -> 120 - 100 - 1 = 19\n    \"\"\"\n\n    apple_match = re.search(r'(\\d+)\\s*apples', s)\n    orange_match = re.search(r'(\\d+)\\s*oranges', s)\n\n    apples = int(apple_match.group(1)) if apple_match else 0\n    oranges = int(orange_match.group(1)) if orange_match else 0\n\n    mangoes = n - (apples + oranges)\n\n    return mangoes\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "```python\ndef pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array of non-negative integers, find the node with the smallest even value.\n    If multiple nodes have the same smallest even value, return the node with the smallest index.\n    The plucked node is returned as [value, index], or an empty list if no even values or the array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    smallest_even_value = float('inf')\n    smallest_even_index = -1\n\n    for index, value in enumerate(arr):\n        if value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the value is even\n            if value < smallest_even_value:\n                # Found a new smallest even value\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = index\n            elif value == smallest_even_value:\n                # Same smallest even value, check for smaller index\n                if index < smallest_even_index:\n                    smallest_even_index = index\n\n    if smallest_even_index == -1:\n        # No even values were found\n        return []\n    else:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "def pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest even-valued node and its index in an array.\n    Returns an empty list if no even values are found or the array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    smallest_even_value = None\n    smallest_even_index = -1\n\n    for i, node_value in enumerate(arr):\n        if node_value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the current node_value is even.\n            # Update if it's the first even value found or if it's strictly smaller.\n            if smallest_even_value is None or node_value < smallest_even_value:\n                smallest_even_value = node_value\n                smallest_even_index = i\n\n    # If smallest_even_value is still None, no even values were found in the array.\n    if smallest_even_value is None:\n        return []\n    else:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\ndef search(lst):\n    '''\n    You are given a non-empty list of positive integers. Return the greatest integer that is greater than\n    zero, and has a frequency greater than or equal to the value of the integer itself.\n    The frequency of an integer is the number of times it appears in the list.\n    If no such a value exist, return -1.\n    Examples:\n        search([4, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1]) == 2\n        search([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4]) == 3\n        search([5, 5, 4, 4, 4]) == -1\n    '''\n    _validate_input(lst)\n    frequencies = _calculate_frequencies(lst)\n    qualifying_integers = _identify_qualifying_integers(frequencies)\n    result = _find_greatest_integer(qualifying_integers)\n    return result\n\ndef _validate_input(lst: list[int]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates the input list, assuming it meets non-empty and positive integer criteria.\n    This function adheres to the design's component list while respecting critical constraints against raising exceptions.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n\ndef _calculate_frequencies(lst: list[int]) -> dict[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the frequency of each unique integer in the list.\n    It builds a dictionary mapping each integer to its count.\n    \"\"\"\n    frequencies = {}\n    for item in lst:\n        frequencies[item] = frequencies.get(item, 0) + 1\n    return frequencies\n\ndef _identify_qualifying_integers(frequencies: dict[int, int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies integers that meet the value-frequency criteria.\n    An integer qualifies if its value is greater than zero and its frequency is greater than or equal to its value.\n    \"\"\"\n    qualifying = []\n    for value, frequency in frequencies.items():\n        if value > 0 and frequency >= value:\n            qualifying.append(value)\n    return qualifying\n\ndef _find_greatest_integer(qualifying_integers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the greatest integer among the qualifying ones.\n    If no qualifying integers are found, it returns -1 as specified.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not qualifying_integers:\n        return -1\n    return max(qualifying_integers)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\nimport collections\n\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    '''\n    Sorts a list by alternating between the minimum and maximum remaining values.\n    Returns an empty list if the input list is empty.\n    '''\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    sorted_deque = collections.deque(sorted(lst))\n    result = []\n    is_min_turn = True\n\n    while sorted_deque:\n        if is_min_turn:\n            result.append(sorted_deque.popleft())\n        else:\n            result.append(sorted_deque.pop())\n        is_min_turn = not is_min_turn\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef search(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the greatest integer x in a list such that its frequency is >= x.\n    Returns -1 if no such integer exists.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Count frequencies of each integer in the list.\n    frequency_map = Counter(lst)\n\n    special_integers = []\n    for x, freq in frequency_map.items():\n        # An integer 'x' is special if it's positive and its frequency is >= 'x'.\n        if x > 0 and freq >= x:\n            special_integers.append(x)\n\n    # Return the greatest special integer, or -1 if none are found.\n    if special_integers:\n        return max(special_integers)\n    else:\n        return -1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\nclass Triangle:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates triangle properties and behavior.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, a: float, b: float, c: float):\n        self.sideA = a\n        self.sideB = b\n        self.sideC = c\n\n    def isValid(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Determines if the sides form a valid triangle.\"\"\"\n        # Business Rule 1: Sum of any two sides must be strictly greater than the third side.\n        return (self.sideA + self.sideB > self.sideC and\n                self.sideA + self.sideC > self.sideB and\n                self.sideB + self.sideC > self.sideA)\n\n    def getArea(self) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the triangle's area using Heron's formula.\"\"\"\n        # This method assumes the triangle is valid as per the sequence diagram.\n        s = (self.sideA + self.sideB + self.sideC) / 2\n        area_squared = s * (s - self.sideA) * (s - self.sideB) * (s - self.sideC)\n\n        # Handle potential floating point inaccuracies that might result in a tiny negative value.\n        if area_squared < 0:\n            area_squared = 0.0\n\n        area = math.sqrt(area_squared)\n        return round(area, 2)\n\ndef triangle_area(a: float, b: float, c: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle, return its area\n    rounded to 2 decimal points if valid, otherwise return -1.\n    \"\"\"\n    triangle = Triangle(a, b, c)\n    if triangle.isValid():\n        return triangle.getArea()\n    else:\n        return -1\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\nclass Triangle:\n    \"\"\"\n    Encapsulates triangle side lengths for validation and area calculation.\n    Provides methods to check validity and compute area using Heron's formula.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, a, b, c):\n        self._a = a\n        self._b = b\n        self._c = c\n\n    def is_valid(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if the current side lengths form a valid triangle.\n        Checks positive side lengths and the triangle inequality theorem.\n        \"\"\"\n        if self._a <= 0 or self._b <= 0 or self._c <= 0:\n            return False\n        if not (self._a + self._b > self._c and\n                self._a + self._c > self._b and\n                self._b + self._c > self._a):\n            return False\n        return True\n\n    def calculate_area(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the area of the triangle using Heron's formula.\n        Assumes the triangle is valid; caller ensures validity.\n        \"\"\"\n        s = (self._a + self._b + self._c) / 2\n        # Use max(0, ...) to guard against potential floating point inaccuracies\n        # that might result in a slightly negative value inside sqrt for valid triangles.\n        area_squared = s * (s - self._a) * (s - self._b) * (s - self._c)\n        return math.sqrt(max(0, area_squared))\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle.\n    Otherwise return -1\n    '''\n    triangle = Triangle(a, b, c)\n    if triangle.is_valid():\n        raw_area = triangle.calculate_area()\n        return round(raw_area, 2)\n    else:\n        return -1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/73", "solution": "```python\ndef smallest_change(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum number of changes needed to make an array palindromic.\n    It uses a two-pointer approach for O(N) time complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    changes = 0\n    left = 0\n    right = len(arr) - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        if arr[left] != arr[right]:\n            changes += 1\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n    return changes\n```"}
