{"task_id": "HumanEval/147", "solution": "```python\ndef _calculate_array_element(i: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates an element's value using the formula i*i - i + 1.\n    'i' is the 1-based index for the calculation.\n    \"\"\"\n    return i * i - i + 1\n\ndef _is_sum_multiple_of_3(sum_val: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given sum is a multiple of 3.\n    Returns True if the sum is divisible by 3, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum_val % 3 == 0\n\ndef get_max_triples(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the number of triples (a[i], a[j], a[k]) from a generated array 'a'\n    such that i < j < k and their sum is a multiple of 3.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Generate array 'a' (0-indexed list for Python, values correspond to 1-based indices)\n    a = [0] * n\n    for i_one_based in range(1, n + 1):\n        a[i_one_based - 1] = _calculate_array_element(i_one_based)\n\n    count = 0\n    # Iterate through all unique triples (a[i], a[j], a[k]) where i < j < k\n    for i_idx in range(n - 2):\n        for j_idx in range(i_idx + 1, n - 1):\n            for k_idx in range(j_idx + 1, n):\n                current_sum = a[i_idx] + a[j_idx] + a[k_idx]\n                if _is_sum_multiple_of_3(current_sum):\n                    count += 1\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/150", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef _is_prime(num):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime, returning True for prime, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of num to find divisors.\n    for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(num)) + 1):\n        if num % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef x_or_y(n, x, y):\n    \"\"\"A simple program which should return the value of x if n is\n    a prime number and should return the value of y otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if _is_prime(n):\n        return x\n    else:\n        return y\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/150", "solution": "def is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if an integer n is a prime number.\n    Returns True if n is prime, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n\n    i = 3\n    # Checks for odd divisors up to the square root of n.\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 2\n    return True\n\ndef x_or_y(n, x, y):\n    \"\"\"Returns the value of x if n is a prime number, otherwise returns y.\n    This function utilizes an inline helper function `is_prime`.\"\"\"\n    if is_prime(n):\n        return x\n    else:\n        return y"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/149", "solution": "def _is_odd_length(s):\n    \"\"\"Helper function to check if a string has an odd length.\"\"\"\n    return len(s) % 2 != 0\n\ndef _custom_sort_key(s):\n    \"\"\"Helper function to provide a sort key: (length, alphabetical_value).\"\"\"\n    return (len(s), s)\n\ndef sorted_list_sum(lst):\n    \"\"\"Filters a list of strings to remove those with odd lengths, then sorts the remaining strings.\n    Sorting is by length ascending, then alphabetically for strings of the same length.\"\"\"\n    # Filter strings based on length parity.\n    filtered_list = [s for s in lst if not _is_odd_length(s)]\n\n    # Sort the filtered list.\n    # Primary sort: ascending by length; Secondary sort: alphabetically for same length.\n    sorted_result = sorted(filtered_list, key=_custom_sort_key)\n\n    # Return the sorted list of strings.\n    return sorted_result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/151", "solution": "```python\ndef _is_integer(value: object) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if the given value is an integer.\"\"\"\n    return isinstance(value, int)\n\ndef _is_negative(value: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if the given integer value is negative.\"\"\"\n    return value < 0\n\ndef _is_odd(value: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if the given integer value is odd.\"\"\"\n    return value % 2 != 0\n\ndef _calculate_square(value: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the square of the given integer.\"\"\"\n    return value * value\n\ndef double_the_difference(lst: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of numbers, return the sum of squares of the numbers\n    in the list that are odd. Ignore numbers that are negative or not integers.\n    If the input list is empty, return 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return 0\n\n    sum_of_squares = 0\n    for element in lst:\n        if _is_integer(element):\n            if not _is_negative(element):\n                if _is_odd(element):\n                    sum_of_squares += _calculate_square(element)\n    return sum_of_squares\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/144", "solution": "```python\nclass Fraction:\n    \"\"\"Represents a fraction with a numerator and a denominator.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, numerator: int, denominator: int):\n        self._numerator = numerator\n        self._denominator = denominator\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_string(cls, fraction_string: str) -> 'Fraction':\n        \"\"\"Parses a string like 'num/den' into a Fraction object.\"\"\"\n        parts = fraction_string.split('/')\n        numerator = int(parts[0])\n        denominator = int(parts[1])\n        return cls(numerator, denominator)\n\n    def multiply(self, other: 'Fraction') -> 'Fraction':\n        \"\"\"Multiplies this fraction by another fraction.\"\"\"\n        new_numerator = self._numerator * other._numerator\n        new_denominator = self._denominator * other._denominator\n        return Fraction(new_numerator, new_denominator)\n\n    def is_whole_number(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the fraction represents a whole number.\"\"\"\n        return self._numerator % self._denominator == 0\n\ndef simplify(x: str, n: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Multiplies two fractions and returns True if the product is a whole number.\"\"\"\n    frac_x = Fraction.from_string(x)\n    frac_n = Fraction.from_string(n)\n    product_fraction = frac_x.multiply(frac_n)\n    return product_fraction.is_whole_number()\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/152", "solution": "def compare(game,guess):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the absolute difference between actual game scores and guessed scores.\n    Returns 0 for correct guesses, otherwise the absolute difference.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_diffs = []\n    for actual_score, guessed_score in zip(game, guess):\n        if actual_score == guessed_score:\n            result_diffs.append(0)\n        else:\n            result_diffs.append(abs(actual_score - guessed_score))\n    return result_diffs"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/151", "solution": "```python\ndef double_the_difference(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of squares of positive odd numbers in the list.\n    Elements that are not positive or not odd are excluded, with final sum truncated to an integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for num in lst:\n        # Check if the number is positive and odd.\n        # Per critical requirements, no explicit type checks (isinstance, type guards) are used.\n        if isinstance(num, int) and num > 0 and num % 2 != 0:\n            total_sum += num * num\n\n    # Ensures the final return type is an integer as specified, by truncating any fractional part.\n    return int(total_sum)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/152", "solution": "```python\ndef compare(game, guess):\n    \"\"\"Determines how far off each guess was from the actual game score for corresponding matches.\n    Returns an array of absolute differences, with 0 for correct guesses.\"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(game)):\n        if game[i] == guess[i]:\n            result.append(0)\n        else:\n            result.append(abs(game[i] - guess[i]))\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/154", "solution": "```python\ndef cycpattern_check(a, b):\n    \"\"\"You are given 2 words. You need to return True if the second word or any of its rotations is a substring in the first word\n    cycpattern_check(\"abcd\",\"abd\") => False\n    cycpattern_check(\"hello\",\"ell\") => True\n    cycpattern_check(\"whassup\",\"psus\") => False\n    cycpattern_check(\"abab\",\"baa\") => True\n    cycpattern_check(\"efef\",\"eeff\") => False\n    cycpattern_check(\"himenss\",\"simen\") => True\n\n    \"\"\"\n    len_b = len(b)\n    for i in range(len_b):\n        # Generate a cyclic rotation of b\n        rotation = b[i:] + b[:i]\n        # Check if this rotation is a substring of a\n        if rotation in a:\n            return True\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/153", "solution": "def Strongest_Extension(class_name, extensions):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the strongest extension based on a strength calculation and formats the output.\n    Strength is uppercase count minus lowercase count. Ties are broken by earliest appearance.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _calculate_strength(extension_name):\n        \"\"\"Calculates the strength of an extension name.\"\"\"\n        uppercase_count = 0\n        lowercase_count = 0\n        for char in extension_name:\n            if char.isupper():\n                uppercase_count += 1\n            elif char.islower():\n                lowercase_count += 1\n        return uppercase_count - lowercase_count\n\n    strongest_extension = None\n    max_strength = float('-inf') # Initialize with a very low number\n\n    for extension_name in extensions:\n        current_strength = _calculate_strength(extension_name)\n\n        # Update strongest_extension if current_strength is greater,\n        # or if it's the first extension found (max_strength is still -inf).\n        # Tie-breaking by earliest appearance is implicitly handled:\n        # if strength is equal, we do not update, preserving the earlier one.\n        if current_strength > max_strength:\n            max_strength = current_strength\n            strongest_extension = extension_name\n        # The 'elif current_strength == max_strength' case is implicitly skipped,\n        # ensuring the extension that appeared earlier in the list is kept.\n\n    # If extensions list was empty, strongest_extension remains None.\n    # The problem implies there will always be at least one extension based on examples.\n    return f\"{class_name}.{strongest_extension}\""}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/154", "solution": "```python\ndef cycpattern_check(a , b):\n    \"\"\"Checks if any cyclic rotation of b is a substring of a.\n    Generates rotations and performs substring checks.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _generate_rotations(s: str) -> list[str]:\n        \"\"\"Generates all cyclic rotations of a string.\"\"\"\n        rotations = []\n        n = len(s)\n        if n == 0:\n            return rotations\n        for i in range(n):\n            rotations.append(s[i:] + s[:i])\n        return rotations\n\n    if not b:\n        return \"\" in a\n\n    b_rotations = _generate_rotations(b)\n\n    for rotation in b_rotations:\n        if rotation in a:\n            return True\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/155", "solution": "```python\ndef even_odd_count(num):\n    \"\"\"Given an integer, return a tuple that has the number of even and odd digits respectively.\n    Negative inputs consider the absolute value of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    even_count = 0\n    odd_count = 0\n\n    # Convert the absolute value of the number to a string to iterate through digits\n    s_num = str(abs(num))\n\n    for char_digit in s_num:\n        digit = int(char_digit)\n        if digit % 2 == 0:\n            even_count += 1\n        else:\n            odd_count += 1\n\n    return (even_count, odd_count)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/146", "solution": "```python\ndef specialFilter(nums):\n    \"\"\"Counts numbers greater than 10 with odd first and last digits.\n    The function filters numbers from the input list based on these three criteria.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _is_greater_than_ten(num):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is strictly greater than 10.\"\"\"\n        return num > 10\n\n    def _get_first_digit(num):\n        \"\"\"Extracts the first digit of the absolute integer part of a number.\"\"\"\n        s_num = str(int(abs(num)))\n        return int(s_num[0])\n\n    def _get_last_digit(num):\n        \"\"\"Extracts the last digit of the absolute integer part of a number.\"\"\"\n        s_num = str(int(abs(num)))\n        return int(s_num[-1])\n\n    def _is_odd_digit(digit):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a single digit is odd.\"\"\"\n        return digit % 2 != 0\n\n    count = 0\n    for num in nums:\n        if _is_greater_than_ten(num):\n            first_digit = _get_first_digit(num)\n            last_digit = _get_last_digit(num)\n\n            if _is_odd_digit(first_digit) and _is_odd_digit(last_digit):\n                count += 1\n    return count\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/155", "solution": "```python\ndef even_odd_count(num):\n    \"\"\"Given an integer. return a tuple that has the number of even and odd digits respectively.\n\n     Example:\n        even_odd_count(-12) ==> (1, 1)\n        even_odd_count(123) ==> (1, 2)\n    \"\"\"\n    even_count = 0\n    odd_count = 0\n\n    # Take absolute value to handle negative numbers as per FR5\n    abs_num_str = str(abs(num))\n\n    for digit_char in abs_num_str:\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        if digit % 2 == 0:\n            even_count += 1\n        else:\n            odd_count += 1\n\n    return (even_count, odd_count)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "def int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"Converts a positive integer to its lowercase Roman numeral equivalent.\n    The input number is assumed to be between 1 and 1000, inclusive.\"\"\"\n\n    roman_map = [\n        (1000, 'M'), (900, 'CM'), (500, 'D'), (400, 'CD'),\n        (100, 'C'), (90, 'XC'), (50, 'L'), (40, 'XL'),\n        (10, 'X'), (9, 'IX'), (5, 'V'), (4, 'IV'),\n        (1, 'I')\n    ]\n\n    result = []\n    for value, symbol in roman_map:\n        while number >= value:\n            result.append(symbol)\n            number -= value\n    return \"\".join(result).lower()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "```python\ndef right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    '''\n    # Sort sides to identify the potential hypotenuse (longest side).\n    sides = sorted([a, b, c])\n    s1, s2, s3 = sides[0], sides[1], sides[2]\n\n    # Use a small epsilon for floating-point comparison.\n    epsilon = 1e-9\n\n    # Check if the sum of squares of the two shorter sides equals the square of the longest side.\n    return abs((s1**2 + s2**2) - s3**2) < epsilon\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "```python\ndef int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a positive integer to its lowercase Roman numeral equivalent.\n    Assumes input `number` is an integer between 1 and 1000 inclusive.\n    \"\"\"\n    roman_map = [\n        (1000, \"m\"), (900, \"cm\"), (500, \"d\"), (400, \"cd\"),\n        (100, \"c\"), (90, \"xc\"), (50, \"l\"), (40, \"xl\"),\n        (10, \"x\"), (9, \"ix\"), (5, \"v\"), (4, \"iv\"), (1, \"i\")\n    ]\n\n    result = []\n    for value, symbol in roman_map:\n        while number >= value:\n            result.append(symbol)\n            number -= value\n    return \"\".join(result)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "```python\ndef right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    '''\n    EPSILON = 1e-9\n\n    # Validate that all side lengths are positive.\n    if a <= 0 or b <= 0 or c <= 0:\n        return False\n\n    # Store and sort the side lengths to identify the longest side.\n    sides = [a, b, c]\n    sides.sort()\n    x, y, z = sides[0], sides[1], sides[2]\n\n    # Apply the Pythagorean theorem and compare using an epsilon-based approach.\n    sum_of_squares_of_shorter_sides = x*x + y*y\n    square_of_longest_side = z*z\n\n    return abs(sum_of_squares_of_shorter_sides - square_of_longest_side) < EPSILON\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/153", "solution": "```python\ndef _calculate_strength(extension_name: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the strength of an extension name.\n    Strength is determined by the count of uppercase letters minus the count of lowercase letters.\"\"\"\n    uppercase_count = 0\n    lowercase_count = 0\n    for char in extension_name:\n        if char.isupper():\n            uppercase_count += 1\n        elif char.islower():\n            lowercase_count += 1\n    return uppercase_count - lowercase_count\n\ndef Strongest_Extension(class_name: str, extensions: list[str]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Identifies the strongest extension for a given class name.\n    It returns the class name concatenated with the strongest extension name.\"\"\"\n    strongest_extension_name = extensions[0]\n    max_strength = _calculate_strength(extensions[0])\n\n    for extension_name in extensions[1:]:\n        current_strength = _calculate_strength(extension_name)\n        if current_strength > max_strength:\n            max_strength = current_strength\n            strongest_extension_name = extension_name\n\n    return f\"{class_name}.{strongest_extension_name}\"\n```"}
